_register_sanitizer_callers refuses an unresolved call_construct=="method"
edge unless the callee is qualified (INV-finoh, correct in purpose: binding a
bare x.encrypt() to Fernet.encrypt would falsely sanitize a real flow). Its
permit branch compared a sanitizer's qualified_name against
_extract_callee_name(dst) -- the NAME slot. Production analyzers put an
inferred receiver type in the MODULE slot, so the comparison was
"cryptography.fernet.Fernet.encrypt" == "encrypt": false by construction for
any method-shaped sanitizer, and every shipped sanitizer is method-shaped.
The refusal was unconditional in production and the ADR-0017 barrier arm was
dead at every idiomatic call site.

Consult the module slot via _module_from_symbol_path, which returns "" for
the `external` placeholder -- an untyped receiver still yields no candidate
and is still refused, so INV-finoh's guarantee is preserved, not widened.
The whole qualified name must match: a typed receiver of the WRONG type is
evidence against this sanitizer (pinned by its own test).

BEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE, shipped catalogue, no overrides. The idiomatic probe
  def handler(f: Fernet, token):
      plain = f.decrypt(token); safe = f.encrypt(plain); os.remove(safe)
reported verdict "violated" at "precise" confidence -- a false positive about
code that visibly encrypts -- and now reports "confirmed" with
sanitized_flows=1, the barrier arm firing (barrier_lines=frozenset({7}) ->
False). This is the barrier-arm positive control that did not previously
exist in any configuration.

DIRECTION IS SUPPRESSIVE and was measured, not argued: more sanitizers
register -> more barrier walks -> more False -> more sanitized, and since
PR #214 a False drops the flow from the violation set. New in-repo instrument
scripts/measure-sanitizer-module-slot-ab.py disables the module slot ONLY
inside sanitizer registration so the A/B measures this change rather than a
superset. Four predictions pre-registered before any number was read; all
held. Corpus delta ZERO in both directions across pretix, hypergumbo, kserve
and caddy. The load-bearing row is pretix -- the corpus's only edge that
reaches the gate (a bare `encrypt`) -- which stays REFUSED in both arms.
Positive control differed as required (+4 call sites, +1 barrier walk), so
the corpus zeros are controlled nulls.

Prevention, not improvement: the corpus holds zero plaintext sanitizer call
sites, and the module slot reaches taint only for python (taint_refine's
annotation pinning) and java (#227). Go/rust/typescript cannot present a
module slot at all today.

Also corrects two claims in CHANGELOG entries that this work falsified: the
barrier-arm mechanism ("receiver typing arms the hazard") was the second
wrong account of that zero and is excised in place, and the corpus claim
"caddy and express are genuinely absent" was wrong for the same reason as
the one it replaced -- both are present under ~/ALL_REPOS/curriculum_repos.

